== Redeemed for Glory

=== §. 48. #emph[Of the inference of glorification from justification and sanctification.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:27.]

    #text(style: "italic")[That He might present her to Himself a glorious church, etc.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[The] chief end, in regard of the church’s good, at which Christ aimed when he gave himself for her, is her glorious state in heaven: this is the end of the end named before. For why did Christ give himself for the church? “That He might sanctify her, having cleansed her.” Why did he cleanse and sanctify her? “That He might present her to Himself a glorious church.” From this, note these three points.

+ #emph[Justification and sanctification must go before glorification.]
+ #emph[The end for which the saints are cleansed and sanctified in this world is that they may be presented glorious to Christ in the world to come.]
+ #emph[The only means to make us glorious before Christ our spouse is righteousness.]

1\. All those places of Scripture which set our righteousness in this world before our glory in the world to come#footnote[Titus 2:12, 13. 2 Tim 4:7, 8.] (as very many places do) prove the first point, that #emph[justification and sanctification must go before glorification.]#footnote[Men must be justified and sanctified before they can be glorified. Rom 8:30.] Among other proofs, note especially the order of the separate links of that golden chain which reaches from God’s eternal counsel before the world to our everlasting glory after this world: “Those He predestined, He also called; those He called, He also justified; those He justified, He also glorified.”

1\. Heaven, the place of our glorification, is “the holy city,” #footnote[#emph[Reasons.] Rev 21:10, 27.] into which “nothing unclean will ever enter.”

2\. In that place the church is to be married to Christ, and to be with him forever. She must therefore be pure, “just as Christ is #footnote[1 John 3:2, 3.] pure,” for he will not endure the company of a foul, filthy spouse.

As we desire assurance of our glorification in heaven, so #footnote[Use.] let us get, and give, evidence of our justification and sanctification on earth. The evidence of our justification is a sound and true faith. The evidence of our sanctification is a good and clear conscience.

The proofs and reasons named before also confirm the #footnote[We are cleansed here so that we may be glorified hereafter.] second point, that #emph[the end for which the saints are cleansed and sanctified in this world is that they may be presented glorious to Christ in the world to come.]

It is therefore needful and fitting, not only in regard of #footnote[#emph[Ex eo quod hic accipit Ecclesia ad illam gloriam quae hic non est, perfectionemque perducitur. Aug. Retract. l.] 1. #emph[c.] 7. \[By what the church receives here, it is brought on to that glory and perfection which is not here.\]] Christ’s honor, but also of our own glory and happiness, that here, while we live on earth, we be sanctified and cleansed. If Christ for our sakes had an eye to our future and everlasting glory, and for that end prepared means to bring us to it, ought not we ourselves much more to have an eye to it, and both avoid all things which may hinder it, and use all means by which we may be assured of it? Moses “was looking ahead to his reward.” Indeed, Christ, “for the joy #footnote[Heb 11:26; 12:2.] set before Him, endured the cross, scorning its shame.”

3\. That #emph[righteousness is the only means to make us glorious] #footnote[Righteousness the only means to make us glorious.] #emph[before Christ our spouse] is evident from this, that Christ gave himself to work and effect this means for this end. Christ himself, by his death, has consecrated this means and no other. If there is any other means than the one which Christ has procured by offering himself up, what need was there for Christ to be offered up? To show that this is the means to make the church glorious before Christ, the Holy Spirit likens “the righteousness of the saints” to “fine linen, bright and pure,” #footnote[Rev 19:7, 8.] with which the wife of the Lamb is made ready for the day of the marriage.

Christ himself has “loved righteousness and hated wickedness”; #footnote[#emph[Reason.] Ps 45:7.] therefore they, and none but they, who are arrayed with righteousness are glorious in his eyes.

I have noted this all the more against the notion of our adversaries, #footnote[Use.] who place all the glory of the church in outward #footnote[Papists place the glory of the church in outward pomp.] pomp. So their Pope, whom they make head of the church, and in a special manner the spouse of Christ, must have his triple crown, his scarlet robes, his throne raised above kings; men must be his horses to carry him, and kings and nobles must be his men to wait on him. Their priests too must be arrayed in glorious copes of the finest worked gold. Their temples must be decked with intricate, carved, gilded images. Their host is carried about in the manner of a triumph. Their people are all sprinkled with water. Their superstitious houses must be the finest buildings in a kingdom, and have the greatest revenues of a kingdom belonging to them; and the like. #footnote[Dionysius Ioui Olympio aureum amiculum detraxit, laneum substituens, non sine dicterio, laneum viz. ad vtramque anni tempestatem esse aptius. \[Dionysius took the golden cloak off Olympian Jupiter and put a woolen one in its place, not without a jest: namely, that a woolen one was fitter for both seasons of the year.\]]

Is this glory fit for Christ’s spouse? Then it seems Christ has carnal eyes and ears, and is delighted with the things that delight the world. The wiser among the heathen scoffed at such base notions as their people had of their gods. Shall Christians think more basely of Christ than the heathen did of their gods? Most people dote far too much on outward, worldly glory, so much that they neglect true righteousness.

For our part, as we desire to appear before Christ in such a way that he #footnote[Use.] may think us glorious, let us be arrayed with righteousness and holiness, “without which no one will see the Lord,” Heb 12:14.

=== §. 49. #emph[Of the enjoyment of Christ’s presence in heaven.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:27.]

    #text(style: "italic")[That He might present her to Himself a glorious church, not having spot or wrinkle or any such thing, but that she should be holy and without blemish.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[Having] noted how this verse follows from the one before, I will now handle it distinctly by itself. It contains a description of the glorious state of the church in heaven. This description must be meant here of that state, #footnote[#emph[Ubicunque commemoraui ecclesiam non habentem maculam aut rugam, non sic accipiendum, quasi iam sit, sed quae praeparatur vt sit, quando apparebit gloriosa. Aug. Retract. l.] 3. #emph[c.] 18. \[Wherever I have spoken of the church as not having spot or wrinkle, it is not to be taken as if it were so already, but as being prepared to be so, when it will appear glorious.\]] for on earth the church is not simply without spot or wrinkle, though it is being prepared to be so.

This state is

+ Set forth in general.
+ Illustrated in particular.

In the general statement are noted

+ Her condition (she is #emph[presented] to Christ.)
+ Her quality #emph[(glorious.)]

The particular illustration of it is

1\. Privative, by removing all deformity: noted in two words, #emph[spot] and #emph[wrinkle.]

2\. Positive, by adorning her with beauty: noted also in two words, #emph[holy] and #emph[blameless.]

The word #emph[(present)] is taken from the custom of solemnizing #footnote[παραστήσῃ.] a marriage: first the spouse was wooed, and then set before her husband, so that he might take her as his wife, to be with him. So Eve#footnote[Gen 2:22.] was presented by God to Adam, so that he might take her as his wife; and Esther#footnote[Esth 2:13.] was presented to Ahasuerus among other virgins. This shows that #footnote[Doctrine.]

#emph[The church in heaven will enjoy the presence of Christ.] #footnote[John 14:2, 3.] Christ himself says expressly to his disciples, “I am going there to prepare a place for you#super[+] … so that you also may be where I am.” On this ground the apostle desired “to depart and be with Christ,”#footnote[Phil 1:23.] and “to be present with the Lord.”#footnote[2 Cor 5:8. #emph[Reason.]]

In heaven the marriage between Christ and the church is solemnized, which has been in preparation here on earth. God the Father has given his #footnote[John 3:16.] Son to the church, and the #footnote[John 17:6.] church to his Son. Indeed, #footnote[Acts 20:28.] Christ himself has purchased the church for himself by his blood, and #footnote[Hos 2:19, 20.] promised marriage to her; and to assure the church all the more of his love, he has #footnote[Eph 4:8.] bestowed many gifts on her. He has further sent #footnote[Paranymphi. \[Friends of the bridegroom.\]] his ministers in his name to #footnote[John 3:29.] woo and #footnote[2 Cor 5:20.] beseech the church to give her consent, and to #footnote[2 Cor 11:2.] prepare her as a pure virgin for himself. On this the church has given her consent, for #footnote[Eph 5:24.] as a spouse she is subject to Christ as to a head. These things being so, how can it be thought that Christ will forsake her, and not receive her to be with him forever?

Can the thought of death be terrible to those who know and #footnote[Use.] believe the truth of this? Will not the consideration #footnote[Encouragement against death.] of it rather make them, with the apostle, sigh and “desire to depart,” that they may be with the Lord? The highest degree of the church’s happiness consists in this enjoyment of the #footnote[#emph[Totum tibi sit Deus: quia horum quae diligis, totum tibi est. Aug. in Ioh.] 3. #emph[tract.] 13. \[Let God be all to you, for he is to you the whole of the things you love.\] Rev 21:22, 23. John 17:20, 21.] presence of her spouse; for so he becomes all in all to her, not by means, as in this world, but immediately, by himself. So there will be no need of any minister, any sacrament, any ordinance to set forth Christ to us; no governor in family, church, or commonwealth to represent his person, or to keep us in subjection; no light to direct us, no food to sustain us: we will be so attended by Christ that we will need nothing. If those servants were happy who stood continually before Solomon,#footnote[1 Kgs 10:8.] what are those who always stand in the presence of one who is infinitely greater than Solomon, not as servants, but as a wife? If it was a great grace and favor that Moses saw the back #footnote[Exod 33:23.] of God, what a grace and favor it is to behold Christ #footnote[1 Cor 13:12.] face to face! For “when Christ appears … we will see Him as He is.” #footnote[1 John 3:2.] Though we are now absent from the Lord, yet let us uphold ourselves with the expectation and assurance of this, that we will be presented before Christ.

=== §. 50. #emph[Of the glory of the church in heaven.]

#emph[The quality of the church in heaven is as excellent as can be,] #footnote[Doctrine.] and therefore it is said here to be #emph[glorious.] All beauty, all comeliness, #footnote[ἔνδοξον.] all grace, whatever may make the church lovely, attractive, or in any way to be desired or admired, is comprised under this word #emph[glorious.] In this respect the saints are said to “shine,” and that “like a most precious jewel,”#footnote[Rev 21:11.] indeed, like “the brightness of the heavens,”#footnote[Dan 12:3.] like “the stars,” and like “the sun”;#footnote[Matt 13:43.] and to be “like Him,”#footnote[1 John 3:2.] Christ himself; and to “appear with Him in glory.”#footnote[Col 3:4.]

This glory of the saints extends to both soul and body, and to the whole person.

In regard of their souls, they will be all “glorious within,”#footnote[Ps 45:13.] for they are “the spirits of the righteous made perfect.”#footnote[Heb 12:23.] #footnote[1 Cor 13:12.] Perfect knowledge, wisdom, and all manner of purity will be in them.

In regard of their bodies, they will be made “like His glorious body,”#footnote[Phil 3:21.] and that in incorruption, immortality, #footnote[#emph[Ecclesia in fine seculi expectat quod in Christi corpore praemonstratum est, etc. Aug. Epist.] 119.] beauty, brightness, grace, favor, agility, strength, and the like. It is therefore truly said that the church at the end of the world expects what has already been shown beforehand in Christ’s body.

In regard of their person, as a wife is advanced to the honor and dignity of her husband, so will they be advanced to the honor and dignity of Christ, as far as they are capable of it: for they will be #footnote[Luke 22:30.] next to Christ, indeed #footnote[John 17:21.] one with him, and so #footnote[Heb 1:14.] above the most glorious angels.

Much more might be said of the glory of the church, but enough can never be said of it, no, not by the tongue of men or of angels: for “no eye has seen, no ear has heard, no heart has imagined, what God has prepared for those who love Him.”#footnote[1 Cor 2:9.] When Paul was caught up into the third heaven, and saw only a glimpse of this glory, #footnote[2 Cor 12:4 ἄρρητα ῥήματα ἃ οὐκ ἐξὸν ἀνθρώπῳ λαλῆσαι.] he “heard inexpressible words, which it is not possible for man to utter.” Therefore, #footnote[2 Cor 4:17.] when he speaks of it, he uses such a transcendent kind of phrase as cannot be fully expressed in any tongue. As well as we can, we translate it thus, by one degree of comparison upon another: #footnote[καθ’ ὑπερβολὴν εἰς ὑπερβολήν.] “#emph[a far more exceeding] and eternal weight of glory.”

Is this not enough to uphold us against all the reproach and disgrace which the world lays on us because we are #footnote[Use.] of the church of Christ? The world has #footnote[Isa 62:4.] of old counted her forsaken and desolate, though Christ calls her “Hephzibah” (that is, #emph[my delight in her]) and “Beulah” (that is, #emph[married]); indeed, it has counted her #footnote[1 Cor 4:13 ὡς περικαθάρματα τοῦ κόσμου, πάντων περίψημα.] “the scum of the earth, the refuse of the world.” Among the heathen, none were so vilely regarded as Christians; among papists, none so much as Protestants; and among carnal professors of the gospel, none so much as those who endeavor #footnote[1 John 3:3.] to purify themselves, “just as Christ is pure,” and to avoid the common sins of the world. When we are basely regarded for Christ’s sake, let us think of this.

=== §. 51. #emph[Of the church’s freedom from all deformity in heaven. Not having spot or wrinkle or any such thing.]

The first point noted by the apostle in his illustration of the glory named before is the removing of all deformity. The word translated #footnote[σπίλος.] #emph[spot] is used for a stain on a garment, and for a foul speck on a man’s face or another part of the body, or a scar or other blemish in his flesh from a sore, wound, blow, or the like. The other word#footnote[ῥυτίς.] (#emph[wrinkle)] is used for a #emph[crease] in the face through old age, for it signifies a #emph[gathering together of the skin by old age.] By it is meant any kind of breaking (as we say) through age, sickness, trouble, pain, or the like. Because there may also be deformities in other ways, the apostle adds this clause #emph[(or any such thing.)] These things, applied to #footnote[ἤ τι τῶν τοιούτων.] the church, show that

#emph[No manner of deformity will cling to the church in heaven.] #footnote[Doctrine.] There will be in her no stain or contagion of sin received from others, no scar of any evil humor arising from itself, no wrinkle, no lack of spiritual moisture, no sign of the old man, nor anything that may in any way make it seem deformed or uncomely in the sight of Christ. Not only great, heinous, capital sins (which are like ulcers and boils, and like open, wide sores, gashes and wounds), but all spots and specks, all wrinkles and defects, all manner of blemishes whatever, within or without, will be taken clean away. Sin will not only be subdued in us, but utterly rooted out of us: no relic, no sign of it will be left remaining. In this respect it is said that “God will wipe away every tear,”#footnote[Rev 7:17; 21:4.] that is, he will take away all cause of mourning, sorrow, and grief. Now there is nothing that gives the saints more cause of sorrow than sin. The remnant of sin which was in the apostle, even after his regeneration, made him cry out thus: “What a wretched man I am!” #footnote[Rom 7:24.]

Though this is only a privative good, yet it adds much to the heavenly happiness of the saints. If it were possible for us to enjoy the rest and glory prepared for the saints in heaven while the spots and wrinkles of sin still remained on us, these spots and wrinkles would be like the handwriting which appeared to Belshazzar#footnote[Dan 5:5.] in the midst of his revelry; they would be like gall mixed with wine; they would turn all our joy into heaviness, and take away the sweet relish of all our happiness. Therefore the consideration of this privative benefit cannot but breed, in the hearts of all who are members of this church, a longing desire for this perfect purging of them from all deformity.

=== §. 52. #emph[Of the perfect purity of the church in heaven.]

But that she should be holy and without blemish.

The last branch by which the heavenly glory of the church is set forth is its perfect purity. The adversative particle (BUT) shows that the holiness spoken of here is no imperfect #footnote[ἀλλά.] holiness, such as the sanctification of the saints is in this world, but an absolutely perfect holiness in all its parts and degrees: such as is without #emph[spot] or #emph[wrinkle,] without relic or sign of sin. And therefore, by way of explanation, there is added #footnote[ἄμωμος.] #emph[without blemish,] or #emph[blameless:] such that neither man, angel, nor God himself can find fault with it. #footnote[Heb 9:14.] This attribute is often applied to the person and blood of Jesus Christ,#footnote[1 Pet 1:19.] and therefore it must set forth perfect purity. From this we may observe that

#emph[The sanctification of the saints will be perfect in heaven.] They will not only be justified by having their sins covered, nor only have their sanctification truly begun in them, but they will also have it absolutely perfected in every part, point, and degree. In this respect they are said to be “the righteous made #footnote[Heb 12:23.] perfect.” Adam in his innocence was not more pure than the saints will be in heaven; indeed, they will far surpass Adam, as in the measure of it, so in its stability and perpetuity.

In our endeavor after holiness let us keep an eye on this #footnote[Use.] perfection, and not lose heart if we do not attain the measure which we desire. Perfection is reserved for the world to come. Yet let us know that the more holy and blameless we are, the nearer we come to that heavenly state; and the more spots and blemishes of sin we have, the more unlike it we are, and the less hope we have of enjoying that heavenly happiness.

All the separate points of the glorious state of #footnote[General uses of the glorious state of the church in heaven.] the church in heaven named before should ravish our spirits, and even break our hearts with a holy wonder at Christ’s goodness, and fill our mouths with praises for it, and make us sigh and long for it, and with all good conscience and diligence use all the means we can to attain it: no labor will be lost in this. Surely this is either not known, or not believed, or not remembered, or not duly and seriously considered by those who make light of it. Let what has been only briefly touched on here be meditated on further, and let us pray that “the eyes of our heart may be enlightened, #footnote[Eph 1:18.] so that we may know … the riches of the glorious inheritance of the saints.” If it were not for this hope, the saints would be #footnote[1 Cor 15:19.] the most miserable of all; whereas now they are the most happy.
